+# Forking Base locally
+
+Short version: `yarn fork base` does **not** give you Base. It gives you a fork of
+Ethereum mainnet, and `cast chain-id` cannot tell the difference. Use
+`yarn fork FORK_URL=base` and verify with a Base-specific read.
+
+## What the onboarding note gets wrong
+
+### 1. The `base` argument never reaches anvil
+
+`packages/foundry/package.json`:
+
+```json
+"fork": "make fork FORK_URL=mainnet"
+```
+
+Before the fix in this checkout that line read:
+
+```json
+"fork": "make fork FORK_URL=${1:-mainnet}"
+```
+
+`${1:-mainnet}` looks like it picks up the word you typed after `yarn fork`, but
+Yarn 4's shell does not bind script arguments to positional parameters — it
+appends them to the end of the command line. Probed in this checkout with a
+throwaway script:
+
+```
+$ yarn _p3 base      # script body: node -e "console.log(JSON.stringify(process.argv))"
+["/…/bin/node","base"]     # 'base' appended as an extra argv, not bound to $1
+
+$ yarn _p4 base      # script body: echo "one=[$1]"
+Unbound argument #1        # $1 does not exist
+```
+
+So `$1` is unset, `${1:-mainnet}` falls back to `mainnet`, and the word `base`
+lands after the make goal as a second goal:
+
+```
+make fork FORK_URL=mainnet base
+```
+
+`fork` is the first goal, and its recipe (`anvil`) blocks forever, so make never
+reaches the `base` goal and never prints the error that would have given the game
+away (`make: *** No rule to make target 'base'. Stop.`).
+
+Running the note's command verbatim in this checkout:
+
+```
+$ yarn fork base
+…
+anvil --fork-url mainnet --chain-id 31337
+```
+
+That is the whole bug. The node you get is a fork of Ethereum mainnet.
+
+### 2. Why the USDC reads come back empty
+
+`packages/foundry/Makefile`:
+
+```make
+# Start a fork
+fork: setup-anvil-wallet
+	anvil --fork-url ${FORK_URL} --chain-id 31337
+```
+
+`FORK_URL=mainnet` resolves through `packages/foundry/foundry.toml`:
+
+```toml
+mainnet = "https://eth-mainnet.alchemyapi.io/v2/${ALCHEMY_API_KEY}"
+base    = "https://mainnet.base.org"
+```
+
+`0x833589fCD6eDb6E08f4c7C32D4f71b54bdA02913` is Base's USDC. On Ethereum mainnet
+that address has no code. A fork of mainnet therefore answers every read against
+it with `0x` — which ABI-decodes to nothing, or reverts, exactly as if the token
+were not deployed. Meanwhile the node itself is perfectly healthy: blocks mine,
+transactions succeed, your own deployments work. Only the address you care about
+is empty.
+
+### 3. Why the chain-id sanity check proved nothing
+
+The Makefile pins `--chain-id 31337` on every fork. The forked chain's real id is
+thrown away. A `cast chain-id` against the local node returns `31337` whatever you
+forked — mainnet, Base, or nothing at all.
+
+Measured here, on a genuine, working Base fork:
+
+```
+$ cast chain-id --rpc-url http://127.0.0.1:8546
+31337
+```
+
+And against the plain (non-fork) node that happened to be running on 8545 in this
+checkout:
+
+```
+$ cast chain-id --rpc-url http://127.0.0.1:8545
+31337
+$ cast rpc eth_getCode 0x833589fCD6eDb6E08f4c7C32D4f71b54bdA02913 latest --rpc-url http://127.0.0.1:8545
+"0x"
+```
+
+Same answer from a correct Base fork, a mainnet fork, and a node that is not a
+fork at all. "I got a number back, so the fork is healthy" does not follow, which
+is how the second failure got misattributed to our contract.
+
+## The command that actually forks Base
+
+```bash
+yarn fork FORK_URL=base
+```
+
+Yarn appends `FORK_URL=base` to `make fork FORK_URL=mainnet`, and make's
+last-wins rule for command-line variables makes the override stick:
+
+```
+$ make -n fork FORK_URL=mainnet FORK_URL=base | tail -1
+anvil --fork-url base --chain-id 31337
+```
+
+Equivalent, and clearer if you prefer not to rely on that:
+
+```bash
+cd packages/foundry && make fork FORK_URL=base
+```
+
+`base` is the alias from `[rpc_endpoints]` in `foundry.toml`; anvil resolves it to
+`https://mainnet.base.org`. No `ALCHEMY_API_KEY` needed — unlike the `mainnet`
+alias, which is why a missing/bad key surfaces as a *mainnet* error message and
+adds to the confusion.
+
+Note the `fork` recipe does not take a `--port`, so anvil uses 8545. If something
+else is already bound there (`yarn chain`, a stray node), anvil exits with
+`Address already in use` — check for a stale listener before blaming the fork.
+
+## The check that would have caught this
+
+Ask the fork a question only Base can answer. Read the token you actually depend
+on:
+
+```bash
+cast call 0x833589fCD6eDb6E08f4c7C32D4f71b54bdA02913 "symbol()(string)" --rpc-url http://127.0.0.1:8545
+cast call 0x833589fCD6eDb6E08f4c7C32D4f71b54bdA02913 "name()(string)"   --rpc-url http://127.0.0.1:8545
+```
+
+On a real Base fork (verified here on port 8546):
+
+```
+"USDC"
+"USD Coin"
+```
+
+On a mainnet fork, on a chain-id-spoofed anything-else, or on a non-fork node,
+that address has no code and the call returns nothing / fails to decode. This
+distinguishes *Base* from *some other chain*, which `cast chain-id` cannot do,
+and it tests the exact precondition our contract needs.
+
+Confirm what the node is actually forked from:
+
+```bash
+cast rpc anvil_nodeInfo --rpc-url http://127.0.0.1:8545 | jq .forkConfig
+```
+
+On the verified Base fork:
+
+```json
+{
+  "forkUrl": "https://mainnet.base.org",
+  "forkBlockNumber": 51663971,
+  "forkRetryBackoff": 1000
+}
+```
+
+A node that is not a fork has no `forkConfig` — and a non-anvil node (Hardhat's
+EDR, say) rejects `anvil_nodeInfo` outright, which is itself a useful signal that
+you are not talking to the node you think you are.
+
+If you want to check the upstream endpoint before starting anvil:
+
+```bash
+cast chain-id --rpc-url base   # => 8453
+```
+
+`8453` is Base. This is the only place a real chain id survives; once anvil starts
+with `--chain-id 31337`, it is gone.
+
+## Change made in this checkout
+
+`packages/foundry/package.json` — the `fork` script no longer pretends to accept a
+positional argument:
+
+```diff
+-"fork": "make fork FORK_URL=${1:-mainnet}",
++"fork": "make fork FORK_URL=mainnet",
+```
+
+Default behaviour is unchanged (`yarn fork` still forks mainnet); the difference is
+that `yarn fork base` no longer looks like it works. Use `yarn fork FORK_URL=base`.
